     SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0

     ★ THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE AN RRO IN values/ LOSES TO THE TARGET'S OWN
     values-sw600dp/, AND LOSES SILENTLY.

     ── How it was found ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
     config_showUserSwitcherByDefault was added to ../values/config.xml, the
     build was green, idmap2 mapped the resource, and the lock-screen avatar was
     still there. `cmd overlay lookup` in verbose mode said exactly why (the
     flags below are described rather than typed throughout this file, because a
     double hyphen is illegal inside an XML comment and aapt2 reports only
     "not well-formed (invalid token)" with no reason — HANDOFF fact 24, and it
     cost this change one build):

         Resolution for 0x07020008
           For config - ...-sw1095dp-...
           Found initial: /vendor/overlay/FrameworkOverlayMalbec.apk #6
         Best matching is from sw600dp configuration of android
         true

     Compare a resource from the same overlay that DOES work:

         Resolution for 0x07040000   (config_defaultNightMode)
           Found initial: /vendor/overlay/FrameworkOverlayMalbec.apk #6
           Overlaid:      /vendor/overlay/FrameworkOverlayMalbec.apk #6
         Best matching is from default configuration of android.overlay.malbec
         1

     ⚠️ **The tell is the "Overlaid:" line.** When it is absent, the overlay was
     found and then out-matched. "Found initial:" alone is NOT success.

     ── The rule ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Config specificity is compared across ALL packages, target and overlays
     together, before overlay precedence is applied. So an override only takes
     effect at a configuration at least as specific as the target's own best
     match. This device is sw1095dp, so every -sw600dp qualifier applies to it,
     and any resource the target also declares under values-sw600dp/ must be
     overridden HERE as well as (or instead of) in values/.

     ── This is a THIRD way for an RRO to be a silent no-op ────────────────
     The tree already documents overriding a resource name the target does not
     have (session 21, config_show_advanced_refresh_rate). This is different:
     the name and overlay order are right, but the unqualified value is simply
     never consulted on this large-screen configuration.

     ── The audit, so this is not rediscovered one resource at a time ──────
     Every resource our six overlays declare was checked against qualified
     variants in its target. Three are shadowed and only one mattered:

       config_showUserSwitcherByDefault  values-sw600dp   <- broken, fixed here
       config_keyguardUserSwitcher       values-sw600dp   <- shadowed, but the
           target ships false in BOTH values/ and values-sw600dp/ and our value
           is false too, so nothing ever differed. Restated here anyway, so the
           line means what its comment says at the config this device runs at.
       status_bar_height_default         values-sw1500dp-110dpi,
                                         values-sw1900dp-90dpi
           Not reachable: this device is sw1095dp, so neither qualifier applies.

     One more, outside this overlay and NOT fixed: SettingsProvider's
     def_device_name_simple is declared in ~80 locale directories. Our override
     wins at en-US because no values-en exists, but a device first booted in e.g.
     zh-rCN would take the target's string and be named by its model code. The
     seed happens once, at first boot, and this device is provisioned in English.
     Recorded rather than fixed — adding 80 locale directories to say one name
     costs more than the defect.

     Reproduce the check with `adb shell cmd overlay lookup`, in verbose mode,
     against `android` and the fully qualified resource name, and require the
     "Overlaid:" line — not just "Found initial:".
-->
<resources>
    <!-- The reasoning for both values is in ../values/config.xml, above the same
         two names. They are restated here for the configuration this device
         actually runs at; see the header above for why that is not redundant. -->
    <bool name="config_keyguardUserSwitcher">false</bool>
    <bool name="config_showUserSwitcherByDefault">false</bool>
</resources>
